capability route/trailing-slash  a path with or without its last `/` sent to the one a route takes
  heroes  web.Router, web.not_routed
  go      gin.Engine.RedirectTrailingSlash, chi/middleware.RedirectSlashes, chi/middleware.StripSlashes, echo/middleware.AddTrailingSlash, echo/middleware.RemoveTrailingSlash, net/http.ServeMux's redirect to a subtree
  rust    -
  python  werkzeug.routing.Rule's strict_slashes, flask.Flask.url_map.strict_slashes
  note    off unless the router asks; 308, which keeps the method and the body, as Flask answers, where Gin answers 301 for GET and 307 for the others

capability route/clean-path  a path holding `.`, `..` or an empty segment sent to its clean form
  heroes  web.route, web.not_routed
  go      net/http.ServeMux's redirect (observed 307 for `/a/../users/7` and `//users/7`), gin.Engine.RedirectFixedPath, gin.Engine.RemoveExtraSlash, chi/middleware.CleanPath
  rust    -
  python  werkzeug.routing.Map's merge_slashes
  note    `route` refuses such a path, so no route's code ever sees one, and `not_routed` answers 308 to the clean path, where Go answers 307 (observed): the clean form does not change, and 308 keeps the method

